## 1️⃣ Gyeongbokgung Palace  
**Slug (API ID):** `gyeongbokgung-palace-seoul`  

### Quick facts
| Item | Details |
|------|---------|
| **Location** | 161 Saemunan‑ro, Jongno‑gu, Seoul (near Gwanghwamun Station, Line 5) |
| **Opening hours** | 09:00 – 18:00 (last entry 17:00). Closed on Tuesdays. |
| **Admission** | Adults KRW 3,000 (~ USD 2.30); Children (6‑12 yr) KRW 1,500; Children under 6 free. |
| **Guided options** | Free English audio guide (QR code) + scheduled live tours (Korean/English). |
| **Family‑friendly features** | • Wide courtyards for running • Changing‑of‑the‑Guard ceremony (10 am & 2 pm) • On‑site museum with kid‑friendly exhibits • Nearby National Folk Museum (free) |
| **Top rating highlights** | ★★★★★ “Best cultural experience for families”, “Well‑preserved architecture”, “Great photo spots”. |
| **Website** | <https://english.visitkorea.or.kr/enu/ATR/SI_EN_3_1_1_1.jsp?cid=264236> |
| **Photos** | ![Palace 1](https://example.com/gyeongbokgung1.jpg) ![Palace 2](https://example.com/gyeongbokgung2.jpg) |

### Must‑see highlights
1. **Geunjeongjeon Hall** – the throne hall where kings were crowned.  
2. **Gyeonghoeru Pavilion** – a beautiful pond‑surrounded pavilion (kids love the swans).  
3. **Changing‑of‑the‑Guard ceremony** – colorful traditional uniforms and music.  
4. **Royal Secret Garden (Huwon)** – short shuttle‑bus ride (extra KRW 5,000) to a tranquil garden.  
5. **Han‑bok rental** – dress up in traditional Korean clothing (≈ KRW 30,000) for memorable photos.

### Practical tips for families
- **Arrive early** (≈ 09:30) to beat crowds and catch the first guard ceremony.  
- **Stroller‑friendly** – flat, paved grounds.  
- **Snack** – bring a light snack; cafés are just outside the main gate.  
- **Combine with nearby sights**: Gwanghwamun Square, Cheonggyecheon Stream, and the Seoul Museum of History (all within a 10‑minute walk).

---

## 2️⃣ N Seoul Tower (Namsan Tower)  
**Slug (API ID):** `n-seoul-tower-seoul`  

### Quick facts
| Item | Details |
|------|---------|
| **Location** | 105 Namsangongwon‑ro, Yongsan‑gu, Seoul (on Namsan Mountain) |
| **Opening hours** | 10:00 – 23:00 (observation deck closes 22:30). |
| **Admission** | Observation Deck: Adults KRW 13,000; Children (3‑12) KRW 9,000. |
| **Cable‑car ticket** | Round‑trip: Adults KRW 9,500; Children KRW 6,500 (includes tower entry). |
| **Family‑friendly features** | • “Locks of Love” fence (kids can attach a small padlock) • Interactive digital “Sky‑Art” gallery • Kids’ AR‑mirror play zone • On‑site gift shop with Korean souvenirs |
| **Top rating highlights** | ★★★★★ “Best city view”, “Great for kids”, “Romantic yet family‑friendly”. |
| **Website** | <https://www.nseoultower.co.kr/eng/> |
| **Photos** | ![Tower 1](https://example.com/nseoultower1.jpg) ![Tower 2](https://example.com/nseoultower2.jpg) |

### Must‑see highlights
1. **360° Observation Deck** – floor‑to‑ceiling glass windows; binoculars available.  
2. **Digital Sky‑Art Gallery** – immersive light‑show installations that change nightly.  
3. **Locks of Love fence** – attach a padlock and write a wish (a favorite family ritual).  
4. **Namsan Cable Car** – scenic ride up the mountain (optional but fun for kids).  
5. **Revolving restaurant “N Grill”** – fine‑dining with panoramic views (reserve in advance).

### Practical tips for families
- **Take the cable car** (or N‑bus #02) to avoid the steep hike; the ride itself is a fun experience.  
- **Visit around sunset** (≈ 17:30‑18:30 in early November) for the best lighting and city‑lights view.  
- **Dress warmly** – the tower’s altitude (236 m) makes it cooler than downtown Seoul in November.  
- **After the tower**, explore Namsan Park’s walking trails and playgrounds for a relaxed end to the day.

